# Shell scripts MUST reach macOS with LF endings.
#
# A CRLF shebang fails as "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M", which reads like a
# broken script rather than a line-ending problem and costs an hour to work out.
# core.autocrlf is a per-machine setting (it is `true` on the Windows box these
# are authored on), so without this the outcome depends on how each clone
# happens to be configured. Pin it instead.
*.command  text eol=lf
*.sh       text eol=lf

# The Windows launcher is the mirror image: a .bat wants CRLF.
*.bat      text eol=crlf

# Icon assets are binary. Git's own detection would almost certainly get this
# right on its own, but core.autocrlf is `true` on the box these are authored on
# and it acts on anything Git thinks is text -- and a PNG whose 0x0A bytes have
# been rewritten is corrupt in a way that surfaces only as "Windows will not load
# the icon", on a machine the author is not sitting at. Pin it, like the rest.
*.png      binary
*.ico      binary

# The .svg files live only in "Intellex logo design/" -- the app reads the PNG and
# .ico builds, because every .svg in that set has an <image> element with no href
# and so draws no R2. Pinned to LF anyway: they are text, they are the master art,
# and a set regenerated on the Mac should not come back as a whole-file diff.
*.svg      text eol=lf
